```rust
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: f64 },
    Rectangle { width: f64, height: f64 },
}

fn area(shape: &Shape) -> f64 {
    match shape {
        Shape::Circle { radius } => std::f64::consts::PI * radius * radius,
        Shape::Rectangle { width, height } => width * height,
        // no default/else needed -- every variant is handled
    }
}
```

`match` here isn't just a fancier `switch` — inside each arm, the compiler knows *exactly* which fields are available, because it's already determined which alternative you're in. Inside the `Circle` arm, only `radius` exists; inside `Rectangle`, only `width` and `height` exist — there's no `width` field to accidentally misuse while you're in the `Circle` branch, because the type simply doesn't have one there.

This is the concrete mechanism behind "pattern matching over a sum type": each arm both narrows *which* alternative you're handling and, simultaneously, narrows *which fields are legally accessible* inside that arm — the compiler enforces both at once, from the type alone.
